{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Strong Induction.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Strong Induction.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"strong induction strong induction","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Strong Induction.md","fileName":"Strong Induction.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Strong Induction","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-16 22:54\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Proof Methods and Recursive Definitions]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Proof Methods and Recursive Definitions","Mathematical Induction","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Strong Induction.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Strong Induction.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"strong induction strong induction","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Strong Induction.md","fileName":"Strong Induction.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Strong Induction","heading":"Strong Induction","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Strong induction proves P(k + 1) under the assumption that every earlier case P(0), P(1), …, P(k) is true. With a suitable base case, this establishes P(n) for every natural number.\n\nThe stronger induction hypothesis is natural when the next object is assembled from components of several smaller sizes, as in prime factorizations or recursively structured trees. It has the same proving power as ordinary [[Mathematical Induction]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Proof Methods and Recursive Definitions","Mathematical Induction","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Strong Induction.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Strong Induction.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"strong induction strong induction","filePath":"6 - F